00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.780
Imagine you bought an energy stock this morning on what looked like a breakout above its 200 day moving average

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.440
after oil futures climbed.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.180
It stalled at resistance and gave back 3% by afternoon.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:49.880
The 200 day moving average often acts as dynamic resistance in choppy macro environments.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.920
The 50 day moving average provides shorter term trend context.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:59.420
Golden crosses and death crosses fail more than 40% of the time in range bound markets.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.820
Support is a price zone where buyers historically step in.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.180
You can see it through multiple bounces from that level.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:08.400
Resistance is the opposite zone where sellers dominate.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:12.700
Both gain reliability went aligned with the 50 day or 200 day averages.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:18.880
The 14 period RSI above 70 signals overbought conditions that often precede mean reversion.

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:21.460
Below 30 it flags oversold bounces.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:25.620
MACD tracks momentum shifts via its signal line crossovers.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:27.800
Volume confirmation matters greatly.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:35.020
If a breakout occurs on 1.5 times or greater the 20 day average volume it succeeds roughly 65% more often.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:40.860
The pro tip most retail investors miss is that professionals always require at least two confirming

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:46.200
indicators. An example is RSI oversold plus rising volume at support.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:51.840
They also respect the higher time frame charts such as weekly versus daily to avoid false signals.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:57.120
The biggest mistake is treating technical analysis as a crystal ball instead of a timing overlay.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:01.700
Always pair it with a fundamental catalyst and predefined exit levels before entering any position.

